---
title: Hooks & Context
description: Read runtime state and control rendering from the FragCanvas subtree.
---

Adapter hooks connect descendant components to one `FragCanvas` runtime. Calling `useSpektral`, `useFrame`, `usePointer`, or advanced user-context hooks outside that subtree throws.

## Runtime context

`useSpektral()` returns:

| Member | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `canvas` | Active `HTMLCanvasElement`, or `undefined` before mount |
| `size` | Readable canvas size in CSS pixels |
| `dpr`, `maxDelta`, `renderMode`, `autoRender` | Writable runtime controls |
| `user` | Namespaced user-state store |
| `graph` | Read-only render graph diagnostics |
| `invalidate()` | Request on-demand rendering |
| `advance()` | Request one manual frame |
| `scheduler` | Stage, diagnostics, profiling, and schedule inspection API |

## Current values

`CurrentReadable<T>` adds a synchronous `.current` getter to a subscribable value. `CurrentWritable<T>` also provides `.set(...)` and `.update(...)`.

Use `.current` in frame callbacks and other imperative code:

```ts
const gpu = useSpektral();

useFrame(() => {
	const { width, height } = gpu.size.current;
});
```

Use `.subscribe(...)` when UI state must react to changes. Avoid creating a subscription for every frame read.

## Graph snapshots

`useSpektral().graph.getSnapshot()` returns the cached render graph snapshot:

```ts
const { graph } = useSpektral();
const snapshot = graph.getSnapshot();
```

This API is experimental until 1.0. The current schema is version 1. Repeated reads return the same deeply frozen object until Spektral builds a new graph plan. A rebuild publishes a new object without mutating the previous snapshot. Before initialization and after teardown, the reader returns the same frozen empty snapshot.

Nodes record execution order, phase, slots, labels, and resource access. Edges include every dependency reason. Physical resource IDs are opaque and valid only for the renderer lifecycle; they are not persistent identifiers. Snapshots contain no GPU objects, WGSL, uniform or storage data, callbacks, or CPU/GPU timings.

## Pointer input

`usePointer(...)` normalizes mouse, touch, and pen events. Its `state` readable includes CSS-pixel coordinates, Y-up `uv` coordinates in `0..1`, Y-up NDC coordinates in `-1..1`, movement, velocity, button state, and inside/dragging flags. Optional click synthesis is exposed through `lastClick`.

`requestFrame: 'auto'` chooses invalidation or manual advance from the active render mode. Use `'invalidate'`, `'advance'`, or `'none'` to override it.

Pointer ownership is single-active: a second `pointerdown` is ignored until the first pointer ends. Disabling the hook or unmounting its component releases pointer capture and resets the active state. Svelte and Vue callers can pass an options getter to update listeners reactively.

## Rendering controls

- `gpu.renderMode.set(...)` switches between `always`, `on-demand`, and `manual`.
- `gpu.autoRender.set(false)` closes the render gate in every mode.
- `gpu.invalidate()` wakes `on-demand` rendering.
- `gpu.advance()` requests one render in `manual` mode and also satisfies the on-demand gate.

See [Hooks API](/docs/api-hooks-reference) for exact options and return types, [Render Passes](/docs/render-passes) for graph routing, [Frame Scheduler](/docs/frame-scheduler) for task registration, and [User Context](/docs/user-context) for advanced namespaced state.
